# f2 = tableBehindTheSum, f3 = listBehindTheSum
# `number + text` whose first reader is `table.get(sum)` is made as a key: found in the table of keys by the number and the text, without a text of the number and without a joining.
# Whether the reader's receiver is a Map or a Set is asked where the sum is walked. Here the receiver is read by key out of a list BEHIND the sum: nothing is built of it yet and nothing is declared of the read:
# what Link says that the read finds answers. The word picks the form in which the text is made and nothing else. A list read the same way is no table: its sum stays a sum.
# RUN: walk f2 mechanisms=all
# CHECK: = Strcat {{*}}[isLikelyKey]
# CHECK: = GetArrayElement
# CHECK: inlineBuiltin=MapGet
# RUN(NEVER): walk f3 mechanisms=all
# NEVER-NOT: isLikelyKey
# RUN(ANSWER): run-in-shell void 0;
# ANSWER: true true true
# ANSWER-NEXT: true true true
# ANSWER-NEXT: false false false
# ANSWER-NEXT: 7@70000,70000@70000 7,70000
# ANSWER-NEXT: 1 -1
# ANSWER-NEXT: exited with 0
